From 6d81d7aaad7ea52c2daad779b56be5a7a1dd6cdc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: whn09 <wanghenan09@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:56:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] vdn: keep the DiT resident at 768p by never building
 the full-canvas clip

768p ran `transformer_before_decode: free` because rank 0's decode peak cleared
80 GiB by 102 MiB with the weights still on the card, so every 768p render paid
2.81 s to evict 45.24 GiB it would have to rebuild (138-232 s) before the next
request. The eviction is not the fix; rank 0's peak is. Two allocations do it,
and both are avoidable rather than necessary:

  * the assembly's own output. `_decode` cats the blended chunks into a
    full-canvas fp16 RGB tensor (1.99 GiB at 345 frames of 1344x768) and the
    caller then denormalises it to fp32 (3.98 GiB) before converting to yuv420p,
    which is 1.5 bytes per pixel. The blend is temporally local and everything
    after it is per frame, so none of that has to coexist: each chunk can become
    its final planes as it leaves the assembly. `parallel.stream_yuv_assembly`
    passes a sink into decode_video_parallel and the only full-length buffers
    left are the planes.

  * the all-gather destination. Gathering every rank's every chunk at once is
    24 pieces x 3 x 22 x 768 x 1344 fp16 = 3.045 GiB, and with the two buffers
    above removed it becomes the largest thing on the card -- it is what the
    first attempt at this actually died on, 3.05 GiB wanted against 2.84 free.
    Gathering one slot at a time holds 0.38 GiB, and costs nothing: slot s
    across the ranks in rank order IS chunks 8s..8s+7, which is the order the
    assembly consumes them in, so the gather is not merely split but pipelined
    with the blend. The carried overlap is cloned at slot boundaries (31 MiB)
    because it is a view into the buffer the next gather overwrites.

Neither changes the arithmetic, and scripts/decode_parity.py asserts that rather
than trusting it: fixed latents, one process, serial vs parallel vs streamed, all
three bit-identical at 768p (0 of 1,068,318,720) and 480p (0 of 429,235,200),
with the streamed planes checked against rgb_to_yuv420p(serial). This is the only
form the test can take -- multi-rank denoise is not reproducible run to run
(index_add_ atomics plus all_reduce, ~17 dB PSNR between identical runs), so a
whole-render A-B could never have shown bit-identity.

Result at 768p: decode peak 64.5 GiB reserved, ~1 GiB clear of the non-PyTorch
floor, and `keep` holds for a render. Nothing here is wired into
infer_ulysses.py yet -- decode_parity.py is what verifies it, and it calls the
decode directly -- so the switch is honoured starting with the next commit,
which is also what finds out that the DiT staying resident is only half the
problem.

